Let me walk through the configuration the way I would set it up for a new service that must talk to two databases.

Spring Boot's auto-configuration assumes ONE database and wires one `DataSource`, one `EntityManagerFactory`, and one `TransactionManager`. The moment you need two, you take manual control of all three beans for each database. The pattern is symmetric: everything you define once for the single-database case, you now define twice.

First, the `DataSource` for each database. I bind connection properties from distinct config prefixes.

```java
@Bean
@Primary
@ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.core")
public DataSource coreDataSource() { return DataSourceBuilder.create().build(); }

@Bean
@ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.audit")
public DataSource auditDataSource() { return DataSourceBuilder.create().build(); }
```

Second, the `EntityManagerFactory` for each. Each one scans ONLY its own entity package, so the two databases' entities never mix.

```java
@Bean
@Primary
public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean coreEntityManagerFactory(
        EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder, @Qualifier("coreDataSource") DataSource ds) {
    return builder.dataSource(ds)
                  .packages("com.bank.multidb.core.model")   // CORE entities only
                  .persistenceUnit("core")
                  .build();
}
```

Third, the `TransactionManager` for each, bound to its factory. A `@Transactional` method resolves to one of these, and that is the database whose transaction it manages.

```java
@Bean
@Primary
public PlatformTransactionManager coreTransactionManager(
        @Qualifier("coreEntityManagerFactory") EntityManagerFactory emf) {
    return new JpaTransactionManager(emf);
}
```

Fourth, I wire the repositories. Each database's repositories live in a separate package and are enabled with their own factory and transaction manager references.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.bank.multidb.core.repository",
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "coreEntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "coreTransactionManager")
class CoreJpaConfig {}
```

The `@Primary` marker matters because when a bean is injected by type and two candidates exist (two `DataSource`s, two factories, two transaction managers), Spring needs a tiebreaker. I mark the main database's beans `@Primary` and qualify the others by name.

Now the critical correctness point: transactions. A standard `@Transactional` method commits against ONE transaction manager, hence ONE database. If a method writes to both databases, those are two independent local transactions — if the second fails after the first commits, you have an inconsistency. To make a single operation atomic across both databases you need a distributed (XA/JTA) transaction manager, which coordinates a two-phase commit. XA has real costs (performance, operational complexity, not all drivers support it well), so in practice I often avoid cross-database transactions entirely and design for eventual consistency with a saga or an outbox pattern instead. When I DO span databases in one method, I am explicit about which transaction manager each inner call uses, for example `@Transactional("auditTransactionManager")` on the audit-writing method.

A couple of senior nuances. First, I disable or scope Spring Boot's `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` expectations by providing all the beans myself, otherwise Boot complains about an ambiguous primary. Second, Flyway/Liquibase must be configured per database if both need migrations. Third, connection pools are sized independently per `DataSource`, so I tune each to its load.

